        defendant: [.direct("Did you leave the voicemail?", "\"Yes. I left it. I was angry that night after losing my job.\""), .direct("What were you feeling?", "\"I was hurt and humiliated. I needed to vent, and it came out the wrong way.\""), .direct("Walk us through what happened the day you were fired.", "\"I was called into the office and told I was being let go. I felt humiliated and left in tears.\""), .direct("Why did you leave the voicemail?", "\"I was at home, upset and angry, and I called the store line to vent. I said something stupid and hung up.\""), .direct("Did you mean it?", "\"Not at all. I would never set fire to a place where people work, and I was sick when I found out the manager took it as a threat.\""), .direct("What happened at the store when you were let go?", "\"The manager told me I was done and asked for my keys. I was humiliated in front of coworkers, and I stormed out without saying much.\""), .cross("You said the place was going to burn. How is that not a threat?", "\"I can see why it sounds like one. I did not intend it as one.\""), .cross("You bought lighter fluid the next day, didn't you?", "\"I did, for a grill. I use it for cookouts.\""), .cross("You did not delete the voicemail from the call history, did you?", "\"No. I had nothing to hide. I knew what I said.\""), .cross("Where did you buy lighter fluid after the voicemail?", "\"At a gas station, for a barbecue with my family. It was a coincidence, and I used it to cook.\""), .cross("You said the place would burn. How is that not a threat?", "\"It sounds like one, and I understand why the manager was afraid. I meant it as venting.\"")],
        openings: ("After {D} was fired from a small retail store, {D} left a voicemail for the manager saying, 'This place is going to burn.' The manager was working nights alone and called deputies.", "Weigh the defense proof, which will show that {D} spoke in anger after losing a job, never meant it, and took no step toward harming the store."),
        closings: ("'This place is going to burn.' A fired employee, an 8 p.m. message, and a manager alone at night who had good reason to be afraid.", "Angry words after a firing, with no act to back them up. A person venting is not a person threatening, and the State must prove otherwise."),
        timeline: [(0, "The firing."), (0, "8 p.m.: the voicemail."), (1, "{V} calls deputies.")],
