        headlines: ["Man Swaps Cloned Card at Gas Pump for $100 Fill-Up"],
        law: "cardFraud",
        spec: "Obtained gasoline valued at $100 from {V}, a gas station, by card fraud, on {DATE}.",
        ages: 18...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "{D} used a cloned card at a pump on {DATE} for a $100 fill-up. A station camera showed {D}. The pump is at a busy station on a main road. The swipe was at about 3 p.m.; the bank flagged the card the next day and the clerk shared the video that day.",
        state: [E(.video, 3, "Pump Camera", "{D} swipes a card."), E(.document, 2, "Bank Notice", "The card flagged as cloned.")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Borrowed Card", "{DL} says a friend gave {D.him} the card.")],
        role: "Station clerk",
        exam: [.direct("What can you tell us about the station and how card readers work?", "\"It's a busy station on a main road, with eight pumps. Customers pay at the pump or inside, and our cameras cover each pump.\""), .direct("What did you see at your pump at about 3 p.m. on {DATE}?", "\"{D} swiped a card and filled up for $100. The station is busy, and I noticed because the transaction looked odd.\""), .direct("What happened with the card?", "\"The bank flagged it as cloned the next day. I shared the pump camera video with them that same day.\""), .direct("What does the pump camera show?", "\"{D} at the pump swiping a card. You can see {D}'s face and the car, and the transaction time matches the bank's records.\""), .direct("What did the pump camera show about the transaction?", "\"At about three in the afternoon, {D} swipes a card, pumps about $100 of gas, and leaves. The card number matches one flagged by the bank.\""), .direct("What did the bank tell you?", "\"The next day, the bank said the card was cloned and the charge was fraudulent. We shared the video with deputies that day.\""), .cross("Did {DL} act nervous?", "\"No. {D} was calm and filled up the way anyone would. Nothing in {D}'s behavior stood out to me.\""), .cross("{DL} says a friend gave {D.him} the card, doesn't {D.he}?", "\"I can't tell if that's true. I only know what the camera and the bank report show.\""), .cross("Could {DL} know the card was cloned?", "\"I can't say. Cloned cards look like real cards. A person using one might not know unless told.\""), .cross("Did {D} do anything suspicious at the pump?", "\"No. {D} was calm, filled up, and left. It looked like an ordinary purchase.\""), .cross("How many other cards were used at that pump that day?", "\"Dozens. I can't tell the jury that every card was valid, only that this one was flagged.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} used a cloned card.", "A friend's card."),
        allocution: "\"I'm sorry.\"",
        truths: ("Three cards were in {DL}'s console.", "The friend is unknown."),
        defendant: [.direct("Where did you get the card?", "\"A friend gave it to me and said I could use it for gas. I didn't think anything of it. It looked like a normal card.\""), .direct("Did you know it was cloned?", "\"No. I'd never have used it if I knew. I'd have thought it was my friend's own card.\""), .direct("Why won't you name your friend?", "\"I don't want to cause trouble. I know it makes me look worse, but I can't give you a name I don't want to say.\""), .direct("What did you think when you used it?", "\"That it was fine. It worked, and the receipt looked normal.\""), .direct("What did you do with the card after you filled up at the pump?", "\"I put it back in my wallet. I did not use it again, and I did not know it had been flagged until the bank called.\""), .direct("How many cards were in your console, and where did they come from?", "\"Three. I got them from the same acquaintance in a bag. I did not look at them closely, and I never thought of them as cloned.\""), .cross("You filled up for $100, didn't you?", "\"Yes. It was an ordinary fill-up. I wasn't trying to hide. I paid with the card and left.\""), .cross("You can't prove a friend gave you the card, can you?", "\"No. I can only tell you what happened. I know it sounds thin, but it's the truth.\""), .cross("Your face is on the camera, isn't it?", "\"Yes. I wasn't hiding. If I'd known it was cloned, I'd have stayed away from the cameras.\""), .cross("Three cards were in your console. Why?", "\"Two were my own. The third was the one I used, and I didn't think about it.\""), .cross("You can't name the person who gave you the card. Isn't that convenient?", "\"It's not convenient for me. I wish I could name a name and clear this up.\"")],
        openings: ("At about 3 p.m. {D} used a cloned card at a pump for a $100 fill-up, and the station camera shows {D}'s face. The bank flagged the card the next day. The State will show the video and the bank notice.", "Jurors will also see a person who used a card a friend lent, filled up openly in front of a camera, and had no idea it was cloned."),
        closings: ("A cloned card, a $100 fill-up, and a camera that shows {D} swiping it. A person who uses a card that isn't theirs is responsible for where it came from.", "{D} used a card in plain view of a camera, believing a friend had lent it. The State has not proved {D} knew it was cloned."),
        timeline: [(0, "3 p.m.: the swipe."), (1, "The bank flags it."), (1, "The clerk shares the video.")],
        identified: "Pump Camera",
        mood: .indifferent,
        genders: ["D": .male])

    static let passwordBorrow = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.passwordBorrow",
        category: .cyber,
        headlines: ["Woman Logs Into Ex's Streaming Account and Changes the Password"],
        law: "accessingComputers",
        spec: "Willfully accessed the computer account of {V} without authority, on {DATE}.",
        ages: 18...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "{D} logged into {V}'s streaming account on {DATE} and changed the password after a breakup. The account was a streaming plan the two had shared during the relationship. The breakup came first, the log-in the next day, and {V} was locked out that same day.",
        state: [E(.digital, 3, "Account Log", "The password changed from {D}'s phone."), E(.statement, 2, "{V}", "Was locked out the day after the breakup.")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Shared", "{DL} says the account was shared.")],
        role: "Account owner",
        exam: [.direct("Tell us about your relationship with {D} and the account.", "\"We were together for about two years. We shared a streaming plan and split the cost.\""), .direct("What happened with your streaming account?", "\"The day after our breakup, I was locked out. The password had been changed, and the log shows it was changed from {D}'s phone.\""), .direct("Had you shared that account?", "\"Yes, during the relationship we shared a streaming plan. Once we broke up, I asked that it be left alone.\""), .direct("What did you ask {D} to do?", "\"I asked {D} to leave the account alone and let me handle it. Instead, {D} logged in and changed the password.\""), .direct("What happened the day after the breakup?", "\"I tried to log in and found I'd been locked out. The email on file had been changed and so had the password.\""), .direct("What did the account records show?", "\"The change was made from {D}'s phone at about nine in the morning, the day after we broke up.\""), .cross("{D} paid half of the plan, didn't {D.he}?", "\"Yes, {D} paid half. I do not dispute that. But the account was in my name.\""), .cross("Was the account locked for a long time?", "\"Only a day or so. I was able to recover it, but it was an annoyance and a violation.\""), .cross("Did {D} take anything from the account?", "\"No. Nothing was taken or deleted that I know of. The only thing {D} did was change the password.\""), .cross("Did {D} pay half of the plan?", "\"Yes, {D} paid half each month. I don't dispute that.\""), .cross("Was anything lost from the account?", "\"My watchlist and several saved profiles. {D} didn't delete them, but I couldn't access them.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} took over an account.", "A shared account."),
        allocution: "\"I'm sorry.\"",
        truths: ("{V} had asked for it to be left alone.", "{DL} paid half."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you change the password on {V}'s account?", "\"Yes, I did. It was a shared plan, and I paid half of it, so I thought I had a right to be in it.\""), .direct("Why did you change it?", "\"I was hurt after the breakup, and I was worried {V.he} would cancel the plan I had paid for.\""), .direct("Did you take anything from the account?", "\"No. I did not delete anything or look at anything private. I only changed the password.\""), .direct("Describe the streaming plan for the jury.", "\"We opened it together and I paid half. My name was on the card for half the time and I use it for my shows.\""), .direct("Why did you change the password?", "\"I was hurt and angry after the breakup. I felt like I'd lost everything, and I wanted some control over something.\""), .direct("Did you delete or read anything?", "\"No. I didn't open any messages or delete any profile. I just changed the password.\""), .cross("{V} asked you to leave it alone, didn't {V.he}?", "\"Yes. I should have listened. I was upset, and I did not think.\""), .cross("You logged into an account that was in {V}'s name, didn't you?", "\"Yes. I knew the password because we shared it. I did not think of it as breaking in.\""), .cross("You locked {V} out. How is that fair?", "\"It is not. I regret it deeply. I was angry, and I made a bad decision.\""), .cross("{V} had asked you to leave it alone. Why didn't you?", "\"I know I should have. I was hurt, and I wasn't thinking about what was right.\""), .cross("You locked {V} out of an account that {V} used. Isn't that a form of harassment?", "\"I see how it looks. I didn't mean to harass, and I gave back access when asked.\"")],
        openings: ("The day after a breakup, {D} logged into a streaming account in {V}'s name and changed the password, locking {V} out. {V} had asked that the account be left alone.", "The defense case is that the plan was shared during the relationship and that {D} paid half. {D} changed the password out of hurt, not to steal."),
        closings: ("{V} asked {D} to leave the account alone. {D} logged in anyway and changed the password from a phone, and {V} was locked out the same day.", "A shared plan, half paid for by {D}, and a password changed in the heat of a breakup. This was a relationship dispute, and no one was defrauded."),
        timeline: [(0, "The breakup."), (1, "The log-in."), (1, "{V} is locked out.")],
        identified: "Account Log",
        mood: .indifferent,
        genders: ["D": .female])

    static let ghostKitchenFee = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.ghostKitchenFee",
        category: .whiteCollar,
        headlines: ["Man Sells Fake Food Truck Permit Help to Three Vendors"],
        law: "falsePretenses",
        spec: "Obtained $600 from {V} by false pretenses, on {DATE}.",
        ages: 18...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "{D} took $600 from {V} on {DATE} to arrange a food truck permit. No permit was ever filed. {D} offered to handle the paperwork for vendors at a local food truck park. {V} paid fourteen days before calling the county, which found no filing the next day.",
        state: [E(.digital, 3, "Texts", "{D} promises a permit in two weeks."), E(.document, 2, "County Response", "No permit filing for {V}.")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Delay", "{DL} says the county was slow.")],
        role: "Food truck owner",
        exam: [.direct("Please describe your food truck business and what you needed.", "\"I run a small food truck and wanted to sell at a local food truck park. The park required a county permit, and I didn't know how to file it.\""), .direct("What did you pay {DL} for?", "\"$600 to arrange a food truck permit. {D} offered to handle the paperwork for vendors at the food truck park, and I paid up front.\""), .direct("What did {DL} promise?", "\"{D} texted that the permit would be done in two weeks. I kept the messages. That was the timeline I counted on.\""), .direct("What did you find out?", "\"After fourteen days, I called the county. They found no permit filing for me, and no one had ever applied.\""), .direct("How did you find {D}?", "\"At the food truck park, where {D} was helping other vendors with paperwork. {D} said the fee was $600 and the permit would take two weeks.\""), .direct("What did {D} do after you paid?", "\"Sent a few texts saying it was in progress, then stopped responding. After fourteen days I called the county myself.\""), .cross("{DL} says the county was slow, doesn't {D.he}?", "\"But the county told me there was never a filing. That's not slowness, that's nothing being sent.\""), .cross("Did you ask {DL} for a refund?", "\"Yes, after I learned there was no filing. I'd like the $600 back, and I haven't received it.\""), .cross("Was it clear {DL} would file the paperwork?", "\"Yes. The texts say so. I wouldn't have paid otherwise. I never agreed to wait indefinitely.\""), .cross("Did {D} give you a receipt or a contract?", "\"A handwritten receipt for $600. There was nothing else in writing about the work.\""), .cross("What did the county tell you?", "\"That no application had been filed in my name. They said the file hadn't been started.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} took money for no permit.", "A slow county."),
        allocution: "\"I'm sorry.\"",
        truths: ("The county has no application.", "Three vendors paid."),
        defendant: [.direct("Why did you take {V}'s $600?", "\"I offered to handle the paperwork for a fee. I planned to file the permit. I didn't mean to take money without doing the job.\""), .direct("Why was the permit never filed?", "\"I got busy and the county process was slow. I meant to file it, and I kept thinking I had more time.\""), .direct("Did you mean to cheat {V}?", "\"No. I thought I could get it done. I regret not being more organized, and I'm willing to repay.\""), .direct("Describe your paperwork business for the jury.", "\"I help small vendors with permits and licenses. I charge a flat fee and I handle the forms and the county counter.\""), .direct("What happened with {V}'s permit?", "\"I started the application and then got busy with other clients. I didn't submit it in time.\""), .direct("Why did you stop responding to {V}?", "\"I was embarrassed. I'd fallen behind on several jobs and I didn't know what to say.\""), .cross("You promised the permit in two weeks, didn't you?", "\"Yes, I did. That was too optimistic. I should have said it could take longer.\""), .cross("The county has no record of a filing, does it?", "\"That's what they say. I don't have a receipt for one, and I know that hurts me.\""), .cross("$600 is a lot for a few hours of paperwork, isn't it?", "\"It's what I charge. I never did the work, so I don't deserve it, and I understand why {V} is upset.\""), .cross("Three vendors paid and none got a permit. Isn't that a pattern?", "\"It's a pattern of me taking on too much. I didn't mean to cheat anyone.\""), .cross("You said the county was slow. Did you even file anything?", "\"I started one application and didn't finish. I shouldn't have blamed the county.\"")],
        openings: ("{V} paid {D} $600 to arrange a food truck permit, and {D} promised it in two weeks by text. Fourteen days later the county said no filing had ever been made. The State will show the messages and the county's response.", "The defense will point you to a vendor helper who meant to file but got buried, a county that moves slowly, and a person who never intended to cheat anyone."),
        closings: ("Six hundred dollars paid, a permit promised in two weeks, and no filing ever made. The texts and the county's reply are the case.", "A promise made in good faith, a county process that dragged, and a person who meant to file. The State has not proved {D} planned to keep the money from the start."),
        timeline: [(-14, "{V} pays."), (0, "{V} calls the county."), (1, "The county has no filing.")],
        identified: "Texts",
        mood: .indifferent,
        genders: ["D": .male])

    static let stolenCharger = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.stolenCharger",
        category: .property,
        headlines: ["Hospital Visitor Takes Phone Charger and Tablet From Waiting Room"],
        law: "misdLarceny",
        spec: "Stole a tablet and charger valued at $280 belonging to {V}, on {DATE}.",
        ages: 18...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "A visitor took {V}'s tablet and charger from a hospital waiting room on {DATE}. A camera showed {D}. The waiting room has rows of chairs and a coffee table, and it is busy in the afternoons. {V} was there at 2 p.m., the tablet was taken at 2:15 p.m., and {V} reported it at 3 p.m.",
        state: [E(.video, 3, "Hospital Camera", "{D} takes the tablet."), E(.statement, 2, "{V}", "Left the tablet on a chair for a moment.")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Mix-Up", "{DL} says {D.he} thought it was {D.his}.")],
        role: "Hospital security officer",
        exam: [.direct("Tell the jury about your job and the waiting room.", "\"I'm a security officer at the hospital. The waiting room has rows of chairs and a coffee table, and it's busy most afternoons.\""), .direct("How did {V} report it?", "\"{V} told us the tablet was missing at about 3 p.m. {V} had been in the waiting room since 2 and left it on a chair for a moment.\""), .direct("Where did {D} go?", "\"{D} left through another door. The camera follows {D} to a corridor, and then {D} is out of view.\""), .direct("What did the hospital camera show?", "\"{D} sits down near the chair where a tablet was left and picks it up. {D} also takes a charger from the table and walks out through a side door.\""), .direct("What was the first thing you did when {V} came to the desk?", "\"I took {V}'s statement and pulled up the camera. I asked for the exact time {V} left the tablet, so I could find the right frame.\""), .direct("How did you identify {D} from the footage?", "\"The footage showed {D} clearly, and a nurse recognized {D} as a visitor from that afternoon. I then tracked {D} to the other exit.\""), .cross("Isn't it possible {D} mistook it for {D.his} own tablet?", "\"It is possible. {D} owns a similar tablet, and a waiting room is a place where people mix up belongings.\""), .cross("Did {D} try to hide the tablet?", "\"I can't tell from the video. {D} picks it up in the open and walks off.\""), .cross("Was the waiting room busy?", "\"Yes. The rows of chairs fill up in the afternoon, and many people have bags and devices.\""), .cross("Could {D} have thought the tablet was {D.his} own?", "\"It's possible. {D} owns a similar model, and many people have the same one.\""), .cross("Was {D} wearing anything that stood out on the video?", "\"Not particularly. {D} wore ordinary clothes, which made the identification depend on the face and the time stamp.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} took a tablet.", "A mix-up."),
        allocution: "\"I'm sorry.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} left through another door.", "{DL} owns a similar tablet."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you take {V}'s tablet?", "\"I took one by mistake. It looked just like mine, and I picked it up and walked out thinking it was mine.\""), .direct("Why did you leave by another door?", "\"I was heading to another part of the hospital. I was visiting someone, and I was not thinking about it.\""), .direct("Did you realize you had the wrong tablet?", "\"Later, yes. I noticed it was not mine when I got home, and I felt terrible.\""), .direct("Explain to the jury why you were at the hospital.", "\"I was visiting a relative. I'd been in the waiting room for about an hour and I had my tablet with me.\""), .direct("What did you pick up?", "\"A tablet that looked like mine. I put it in my bag and walked out toward the parking lot.\""), .direct("When did you realize it wasn't yours?", "\"Later, when I opened my bag at home. The tablet had a different case, and I knew it wasn't mine.\""), .cross("You never returned it, did you?", "\"No. I should have, yes. I was embarrassed and I did not know how to get it back.\""), .cross("You took a charger as well, didn't you?", "\"It was next to the tablet. I assumed it was mine too.\""), .cross("You own a similar tablet. Where is yours?", "\"I had left mine at home that day, which is why I did not notice.\""), .cross("You never returned it. Why not?", "\"I meant to, and I was embarrassed. I didn't know how to explain what had happened.\""), .cross("You took the charger too. How is that a mistake?", "\"It was next to the tablet, and I picked up both without thinking.\"")],
        openings: ("A tablet and a charger were taken from a chair in a hospital waiting room at 2:15 p.m. A hospital camera recorded {D} picking them up and carrying them out a side door.", "Jurors will hear that {D} owns a nearly identical tablet and picked up {V}'s by mistake in a busy waiting room, and that {D} never meant to take anything that was not {D.his}."),
        closings: ("The camera shows {D} taking the tablet and charger and leaving by another door, and {D} never gave them back. Taking what is not yours and keeping it is larceny.", "A busy waiting room, a tablet that looks just like {D}'s own, and an honest mix-up. The State must prove {D} meant to steal, and a mistake is not a theft."),
        timeline: [(0, "2 p.m.: the waiting room."), (0, "2:15 p.m.: the tablet is taken."), (0, "3 p.m.: {V} reports it.")],
        identified: "Hospital Camera",
        mood: .indifferent)

    static let lostPackageLocker = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.lostPackageLocker",
        category: .property,
        headlines: ["Resident Opens Apartment Package Locker With a Stolen Code"],
        law: "misdLarceny",
        spec: "Stole packages valued at $250 belonging to {V}, on {DATE}.",
        ages: 18...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "{D} opened an apartment package locker with a code that was not {D.his} on {DATE} and took {V}'s parcels. The apartment complex has a bank of package lockers opened by a code sent to each resident. The code was entered at 4 p.m., the locker opened five minutes later, and {V} reported missing parcels the next day.",
        state: [E(.digital, 3, "Locker Log", "{D}'s fob opened {V}'s locker."), E(.document, 2, "Locker Records", "The log shows the locker opening at 4:05 p.m.")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Mistake", "{DL} says {D.he} mis-keyed.")],
        role: "Apartment manager",
        exam: [.direct("Tell us about the package lockers at your complex.", "\"We have a bank of lockers in the lobby. When a package arrives, the resident gets a code by text and enters it to open the locker.\""), .direct("How do the package lockers work?", "\"Each resident gets a code, and the locker bank opens only for that code. Every opening is recorded in a log.\""), .direct("What did {V} report?", "\"{V} reported missing parcels the next day. The log showed three parcels removed from that locker.\""), .direct("What does the locker log show for {V}'s locker?", "\"At 4:05 p.m., the locker opened after a code was entered. The log shows a fob swipe and a code that didn't match {V}'s.\""), .direct("How many parcels were taken?", "\"Three. {V} reported them missing the next day and showed me the delivery notices.\""), .direct("What did you do when {V} told you a parcel was missing?", "\"I pulled the locker log and saw {D}'s fob opening {V}'s locker at 4:05. I printed the log and gave it to {V} and to deputies.\""), .cross("Did {D} get the code wrong on other tries?", "\"The locker stayed locked on other tries. This one opened on the first try, which is odd.\""), .cross("Could a locker open by mistake?", "\"It should not. The code has to match. I do not know how it opened with a wrong code.\""), .cross("Did {D} return the parcels?", "\"Not that I know of. They were not returned to my office.\""), .cross("Did the code work on other tries?", "\"No. When someone entered a wrong code on other lockers, they stayed locked.\""), .cross("Could a fob open the wrong locker by mistake?", "\"It shouldn't, but I've seen it happen with a faulty reader.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} took packages.", "A mis-keyed code."),
        allocution: "\"I'm sorry.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} took three parcels.", "The locker stayed locked on other tries."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you take {V}'s parcels?", "\"I took them by mistake. I thought they were mine because the locker opened with the code I entered.\""), .direct("Did you check the labels?", "\"Not at first. When I got upstairs, I saw the names. I did not know what to do.\""), .direct("Walk us through how you got into the locker.", "\"I got a code for a package of my own and entered it at the bank. A locker opened, and I took out the parcels inside.\""), .direct("Why did you think they were yours?", "\"The locker opened for my code, so I assumed they were mine. I carried them upstairs without looking at the labels.\""), .direct("What did you do when you saw the labels?", "\"I realized they had a different name. I put them on my counter meaning to return them, and I got busy.\""), .direct("What did you do with the parcels after you took them home?", "\"I put them on my counter and noticed other names on the labels the next morning. I meant to return them, and I let it slide.\""), .cross("You never returned them, did you?", "\"No. I should have taken them back to the office. I was embarrassed.\""), .cross("Three parcels with someone else's name on them. How is that a mistake?", "\"I did not read the labels. I realize how that sounds.\""), .cross("Your fob opened the locker. Is that normal?", "\"I thought it was a glitch. It had never happened before.\""), .cross("You didn't return them. Why not?", "\"I meant to, but I was worried about how it would look. I should have taken them back right away.\""), .cross("Three parcels is a lot to take by mistake. Weren't you suspicious when you opened the locker?", "\"I didn't think about it. I was in a hurry and I saw packages.\"")],
        openings: ("An apartment package locker opened for a code that was not {D}'s on {DATE}, and {V}'s three parcels disappeared. The log shows {D}'s fob and a locker opening at 4:05 p.m.", "The defense evidence will establish that the locker opened for {D}'s code and that {D} took the parcels by mistake, believing they were {D.his} own."),
        closings: ("A fob log, a locker opening, three parcels gone, and none returned. {D} had every chance to give them back and did not.", "The locker opened for {D}, and {D} thought the contents were {D.his}. A mistake is not a theft, and the State must prove otherwise."),
        timeline: [(0, "4 p.m.: the code."), (0, "4:05 p.m.: the locker opens."), (1, "{V} reports missing parcels.")],
        identified: "Locker Log",
        mood: .indifferent)

